544    /** FUNCTION otap_test.match_regex
545    * Checks given data of type VARCHAR2 against an Oracle REGEX expression. Uses REGEXP_LIKE.
546    * ATTENTION Oracle REGEX implementation is not standard. Unix regex which work like charm take hours to implement in
547    * Oracle REGEX to work as desired. Test your expression well with Oracle before using it.
548    *
549    * Easiest way to check is SELECT COUNT(*) FROM dual WHERE regexp_like('your string', 'your regex', 'regex param');
550    * Should result in 1 if successful checked. You may want to prepare a with block with different string to pass them
551    * through the regular expression.
552    *
553    * @param p_have The data to check.
554    * @param p_regex A valid Oracle regular expression that p_have must match.
555    * @param p_description The test description if any. If not given, a description is generated, see template.
556    * @param p_param Parameter for REGEXP_LIKE. 'i' is case insensitive. See Oracle documentation for details, https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/21/sqlrf/Pattern-matching-Conditions.html#GUID-D2124F3A-C6E4-4CCA-A40E-2FFCABFD8E19.
557    * @param p_expected_result The expected test result as number. Default is test passed. See otap_constants.
558    *
559    * @return The test result as text.
560    */
561    FUNCTION match_regex( p_have	       IN     VARCHAR2
562  			   , p_regex	       IN     VARCHAR2
563  			   , p_description     IN     VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
564  			   , p_param	       IN     VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
565  			   , p_expected_result IN     NUMBER   DEFAULT otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TEST_PASSED
566  			   )
567  	 RETURN VARCHAR2
568    ;
